At Ostrander Veterinary Labs (fictional), SampleExpiryTests.MarksSampleExpiredAfter48h in our .NET 8 xUnit suite fails in about 1 of 20 CI runs. Triage ran the suite 300 times and confirmed two causes. (1) ResultCache.Shared is a static Dictionary used by several test classes that xUnit runs in parallel, so samples from other classes sometimes appear in the count. (2) The test waits a fixed Task.Delay for a background sweeper that fires on a System.Threading.Timer every 500 ms.

Current test:

[Fact]
public async Task MarksSampleExpiredAfter48h() {
  var svc = new SampleService(ResultCache.Shared);
  svc.Register(new Sample("S-100", DateTime.UtcNow.AddHours(-49)));
  await Task.Delay(700);
  Assert.Equal(1, ResultCache.Shared.Count(s => s.Value.Expired));
}

SampleService reads DateTime.UtcNow inside its sweeper and creates its own Timer. The test project already references Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing. Rewrite the test, and add the smallest production seam needed, so it is deterministic under parallel runs. Do not change the 48-hour rule. Also give me one concrete way to confirm locally that the flakiness is gone.

